1
00:00:00,503 --> 00:00:02,971
To handle the Yes button, I'll start by

2
00:00:02,971 --> 00:00:03,686
selecting it,

3
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:05,098
the Yes button,

4
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:08,736
with document.getElementById,

5
00:00:09,608 --> 00:00:12,062
and then this button has action-yes

6
00:00:12,675 --> 00:00:13,111
as an ID.

7
00:00:14,726 --> 00:00:15,531
So I'll use that

8
00:00:17,268 --> 00:00:17,590
like this.

9
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:22,272
And then of course I add an event listener

10
00:00:23,604 --> 00:00:25,833
for the click event to this button as well.

11
00:00:26,607 --> 00:00:29,678
But now here, I of course also wanna close

12
00:00:29,678 --> 00:00:30,433
the dialog,

13
00:00:31,189 --> 00:00:33,890
but in addition, I wanna make sure that I

14
00:00:33,938 --> 00:00:35,475
send that request

15
00:00:36,045 --> 00:00:37,723
which I canceled by calling

16
00:00:38,163 --> 00:00:39,108
event.preventDefault.

17
00:00:41,507 --> 00:00:44,254
And that's something that's not that easy to

18
00:00:44,254 --> 00:00:47,371
do if you're listening to the before-request

19
00:00:47,451 --> 00:00:48,511
HTMX event.

20
00:00:49,315 --> 00:00:51,476
Because there, if you then cancel the event,

21
00:00:51,492 --> 00:00:54,742
you're really canceling the entire request.

22
00:00:55,459 --> 00:00:58,133
And you would have to manually recreate it,

23
00:00:58,197 --> 00:00:58,809
but even then,

24
00:00:59,486 --> 00:01:01,951
HTMX wouldn't be handling it anymore.

25
00:01:03,780 --> 00:01:06,823
That's why the better HTMX event to handle

26
00:01:06,855 --> 00:01:07,048
here

27
00:01:07,595 --> 00:01:11,556
instead of before request is HTMX confirm

28
00:01:12,667 --> 00:01:15,834
because this is an event that's triggered or

29
00:01:16,107 --> 00:01:16,541
emitted

30
00:01:17,152 --> 00:01:19,578
after a trigger occurred on an element.

31
00:01:20,686 --> 00:01:21,908
And as you see in the official

32
00:01:21,908 --> 00:01:24,167
documentation, it allows us to cancel or

33
00:01:24,217 --> 00:01:27,341
delay the AJAX request HTMX would be about

34
00:01:27,473 --> 00:01:27,938
to send.

35
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:32,739
So it's HTMX confirm to which I wanna listen

36
00:01:32,771 --> 00:01:35,074
here, which will be emitted whenever

37
00:01:36,330 --> 00:01:38,552
some HTMX request is triggered,

38
00:01:39,695 --> 00:01:41,581
which of course happens here on that button.

39
00:01:43,563 --> 00:01:46,625
And then when we prevent the default, we're

40
00:01:46,786 --> 00:01:49,967
actually not entirely canceling the request.

41
00:01:50,032 --> 00:01:52,151
Instead, we're just stopping it for the

42
00:01:52,151 --> 00:01:53,365
moment, you could say,

43
00:01:54,253 --> 00:01:57,305
That's how HTMX works internally with that

44
00:01:57,305 --> 00:01:57,723
event.

45
00:01:58,911 --> 00:02:00,501
But we can then resume

46
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:03,778
the request or restart the request and

47
00:02:03,794 --> 00:02:07,745
restart this HTMX process of sending the

48
00:02:07,745 --> 00:02:09,624
request and using the response

49
00:02:10,219 --> 00:02:12,965
by using a special method that's part of the

50
00:02:13,270 --> 00:02:14,218
event details.

51
00:02:15,471 --> 00:02:18,908
So here in the yes button event handler, we

52
00:02:18,908 --> 00:02:19,583
can use

53
00:02:21,819 --> 00:02:24,474
event.detail, and there for this specific

54
00:02:24,602 --> 00:02:28,415
confirm event, we now have an issue request

55
00:02:28,463 --> 00:02:28,769
method

56
00:02:29,847 --> 00:02:31,816
made available by HTMX.

57
00:02:33,710 --> 00:02:36,219
And I know about that because the official

58
00:02:36,219 --> 00:02:39,391
documentation tells us that this exists. So

59
00:02:39,472 --> 00:02:41,854
definitely dive into that documentation from

60
00:02:41,886 --> 00:02:44,429
time to time if you're playing around with

61
00:02:44,623 --> 00:02:46,039
different HTMX events.

62
00:02:48,491 --> 00:02:50,375
Now issueRequest will do what the name

63
00:02:50,424 --> 00:02:53,854
suggests. It will issue the request and HTMX

64
00:02:53,854 --> 00:02:55,884
will then still handle the response and so

65
00:02:55,964 --> 00:02:56,158
on

66
00:02:56,834 --> 00:02:57,641
as it always would.

67
00:02:58,948 --> 00:03:00,965
But with that, we therefore then make sure

68
00:03:00,965 --> 00:03:04,032
that we do close the dialog but then also

69
00:03:04,032 --> 00:03:05,937
continue with the intended action.

70
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:08,935
And therefore now with this in place,

71
00:03:09,631 --> 00:03:10,716
if we reload again,

72
00:03:11,719 --> 00:03:13,565
if I add an item, you see I get the dialog,

73
00:03:14,288 --> 00:03:16,083
If I cancel, nothing happens.

74
00:03:16,676 --> 00:03:18,808
If I click yes though, the item is added.

75
00:03:19,482 --> 00:03:21,550
And I can do this for multiple places. And

76
00:03:21,566 --> 00:03:24,050
if I click on an item up here, I get the

77
00:03:24,050 --> 00:03:27,160
same dialog now with the text remove though.

78
00:03:27,737 --> 00:03:29,180
And I can also cancel that,

79
00:03:29,821 --> 00:03:31,152
but I can also confirm it.

80
00:03:32,370 --> 00:03:34,470
And therefore now I got the functionality I

81
00:03:34,550 --> 00:03:37,548
want with help of some custom JavaScript

82
00:03:37,580 --> 00:03:39,968
code. And it's important to know that you

83
00:03:40,145 --> 00:03:43,543
can add your own custom JavaScript code to

84
00:03:43,687 --> 00:03:47,118
HTMX managed or HTMX X Enhanced

85
00:03:47,215 --> 00:03:47,898
applications.
